From eb60bff91c4d32f269f7909bc27d0c77a0a20a64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Phil <300208363+calicopizzadelivery@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 08:41:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Audio output tile: pick the output, not just report it

The tile reported which output was live and then opened Sound settings,
which on this build has nothing to do with choosing an output device. Make
it a picker: the built-in output and every connected Bluetooth sink.

Switching output is activating or deactivating a Bluetooth sink, because
A2DP outranks HDMI in the platform's routing policy -- "use HDMI" means
"have no active Bluetooth audio device". So this is BluetoothAdapter
setActiveDevice/removeActiveDevice with ACTIVE_DEVICE_AUDIO, the same pair
TvSettings already uses from AccessoryUtils.

Reading state stays on public API: AudioManager.getDevices plus
AudioDeviceInfo.getAddress to match the routed sink. Only the switch needs
BLUETOOTH_PRIVILEGED, so a build where that is refused still shows the
right thing rather than showing nothing.

AudioManager only lists a Bluetooth sink while it is the active route, so
it cannot enumerate connected-but-idle devices. The A2DP profile proxy
can, which is why one is bound for the life of the activity. An
AudioDeviceCallback repaints the tile, since routing is asynchronous and a
sink can also come and go on its own.

With one output there is no choice to make, so the tile opens accessory
settings instead of showing a list of one.

BLUETOOTH_PRIVILEGED goes in the privapp allowlist and BLUETOOTH_CONNECT
in the default-permission grants; both are confirmed granted at runtime.

The dialog is pinned to the panel's edge rather than centred, so it reads
as part of the pop-over instead of a separate surface.

Test: lineage_sdk_tv_x86_64. Tile reports Speaker (no HDMI on the
emulator), clicking with one output opens accessory settings, both
permissions show granted in dumpsys, no crash. The multi-device list and
the switch itself are NOT verified -- neither the emulator nor the bench
has a Bluetooth audio sink. The dialog layout was checked with a
temporary stub, since removed.

